        {
          "tipo": "gramatica",
          "min": 12,
          "titulo": "Ordering politely with «queria»",
          "explicacao": "STEP 1: to ask for something in a café, a shop or a restaurant, Brazilians say «queria» + the thing: «Queria um café, por favor.» It means «I would like a coffee, please». It is polite and natural. You can add «eu» («Eu queria…»), but you do not need to. STEP 2: «queria» comes from the verb «querer» (to want). The full verb is lesson 13; for now learn «queria» as a ready-made phrase for ordering. The plain «quero» (I want) is correct, but it sounds more direct, so «queria» is the safer choice with strangers. STEP 3: the thing you ask for needs «um» or «uma» (or a number), and it must agree with the noun: «um café» (masculine), «uma água» (feminine), «um suco», «uma xícara de café». STEP 4: «de» joins a container or a type to its content: «um copo de água», «uma xícara de café», «suco de laranja», «pão de queijo». English often has no word here, but Portuguese always needs «de». STEP 5: the person serving you may ask «Pois não?» (how can I help you?), «Mais alguma coisa?» (anything else?) and «É pra comer aqui ou pra levar?» (is it to eat here or to take away?). You can answer «É só isso, obrigado» (that is all, thanks) and ask for «a conta, por favor». STEP 6: in the morning, Brazilians have «café da manhã» (breakfast), often in a padaria: coffee, bread with butter, cheese bread, juice, fruit.",
          "comparacao": "In English you say «Can I have…?» or «I'd like…». Portuguese «queria» is the same idea as «I'd like». English «a glass of water» needs «of», and so does Portuguese «um copo de água». Do not say «um copo água».",
